      "practice_mapping_verdict": "PASS",
      "summary": "Checked every field against the current 247 CMR 9.00 PDF (12/6/24 pages), the four Board policies on the current Pharmacy Practice Resources list, 247 CMR 2.00 and 8.05, 105 CMR 700, the PMP dispenser guide, M.G.L. c. 94C s. 21, 15 U.S.C. 1473 and 1476, 16 CFR 1700.1/1700.14/1700.15 on eCFR, and CPSC 384. 247 CMR 9.07, 9.08 (three packaging types; multi-drug-single-dose 60-day, Schedule II/III and as-needed limits; same-patient return and repackaging with 9.08(5) relabel and pharmacist record; label and BUD rules), 9.10, 9.11 and 9.12 are paraphrased accurately. Policies are presented as policy with their own modality and stated accurately: 2019-02 (pharmacy ownership, approval determination by a facility without an on-site pharmacy, 2-year onsite records, emergency-kit loader list including nurses with ordinary technicians limited to Schedules III-VI, daily-dispensing loaders, CSR/DEA and video); 2023-08 (on-site pharmacy and MCSR-address threshold, trainee III-VI, other licensed technicians II-VI, CPhT non-PMP Schedule VI without prior verification); 2022-07 (licensing-body approval for facilities, written request before use, narrower consultation hours with the regulation controlling); 2023-01 ('Unless otherwise prohibited', no Schedule II/III repetition, planner additions). Tensions with 247 CMR 2.00 direct supervision and 8.05 Schedule II transport are flagged candidly with a safe course. PPPA content, effectiveness figures (85/80, 80 for unit packaging, 90 SAUE with the aerosol/metal carve-out), exemptions, 1473(b) routes, no-reuse and 1476 preemption are correct, and CPSC material is consistently labelled staff guidance. The Massachusetts no-additional-requirement statement is framed as a dated search and matched my checks of c. 94C s. 21, 247 CMR 9.00 and 105 CMR 700. All 22 practice questions are answerable and not contradicted. Two Low, non-misleading imprecisions noted."
